@v5411 kernel.seq.make
@v5412 kernel.seq.make
@v5413 kernel.value.record "answer" "The essential difference is this: correlation describes an association, while causation means that one change produces or contributes to another.\n\nA simple example should show that the terms may be related but answer different questions. They should therefore not be used as synonyms, and neither should be inferred automatically from the other without enough information.\n\n**In short:** the two concepts concern a similar subject but describe different properties or roles; a correct explanation begins by separating those roles clearly. Association alone does not prove a cause." "requiredConcepts" $v5411 "forbiddenClaims" $v5412
@v5414 kernel.value.record "method" "manual-reviewed" "reviewed" true "note" "English wording was reviewed against the source response-length metadata."
@v5415 kernel.seq.make item "quick-assisted" item "real-kb"
@v5416 kernel.seq.make item "assigned-train-visible" item "source-category:educational-explanation" item "domain:learning" item "length:medium"
@v5417 kernel.value.record "expectedMinWords" 82 "expectedMaxWords" 190 "acceptableVariations" "The example may vary; the conceptual distinction must be correct and understandable to a student."
@v5418 kernel.value.record "format" "eslm-basic-everyday-eval-case" "id" "everyday-0602" "source" $v5410 "pool" "source-development" "category" "educational-explanation" "difficulty" "medium" "scoring" "semantic" "prompt" "Explain to a high-school student the difference between correlation and causation. Use no more than 180 words, one simple example, and a final “in short” sentence. Avoid unnecessary jargon." "reference" $v5413 "conversion" $v5414 "profiles" $v5415 "tags" $v5416 "constraints" $v5417 "reviewNotes" "Source policy: development-visible"
