@v8155 kernel.value.record "collection" "eslm-everyday-source.jsonl" "locator" "dataset.jsonl:907" "sourceId" "RO-091-07" "sourceDigest" "sha256:7f24282c3c01385e3fb03a7243edecde0d5a5b6747ccae64ce17668be3aae033" "sourceFile" "cases/benchmark_091.md"
@v8156 kernel.seq.make
@v8157 kernel.seq.make
@v8158 kernel.value.record "answer" "An organization pays for different things in order to operate, and those expenses do not all change in the same way.\n\nSome costs rise or fall with activity. If a bakery makes more loaves, it will use more flour and packaging. These are **variable costs** because they are fairly directly tied to how much is produced.\n\nOther costs change less in the short term. The rent for the premises, for example, may stay the same whether the bakery makes 500 or 700 loaves in a day. This is a **fixed cost** within the range being considered.\n\n“Fixed” does not mean “small” or “unimportant.” Rent can be a very large expense. Likewise, “variable” does not mean negligible: a small amount of material per product can become a large sum when production is high.\n\nThere are also mixed costs. A service may have a fixed monthly fee plus an amount that grows with usage.\n\nThe distinction helps answer a practical question: **how do expenses change if activity rises or falls?** It describes the cost’s behavior, not whether the cost is good, bad, or important.\n\nThe bakery example shows that one organization can have all three kinds of cost at once. Flour follows output, rent remains stable within a range, and some services combine a fixed fee with usage. The classification also depends on the period examined: a cost that seems fixed in the short term may change over a longer period. This helps estimate expenses without labeling any cost as inherently important or unimportant." "requiredConcepts" $v8156 "forbiddenClaims" $v8157
@v8159 kernel.value.record "method" "manual-reviewed" "reviewed" true "note" "English wording was reviewed against the source response-length metadata."
@v8160 kernel.seq.make item "core-only"
@v8161 kernel.seq.make item "assigned-train-visible" item "source-category:complex-everyday-task" item "domain:decision-and-synthesis" item "length:long"
@v8162 kernel.value.record "expectedMinWords" 246 "expectedMaxWords" 850 "acceptableVariations" "The answer may vary but must follow every constraint, use the supplied data, and not invent material information."
@v8163 kernel.value.record "format" "eslm-basic-everyday-eval-case" "id" "everyday-0907" "source" $v8155 "pool" "source-development" "category" "complex-everyday-task" "difficulty" "medium-hard" "scoring" "semantic" "prompt" "Rewrite the following explanation for a reader with no economics background, using a simple example and no formulas: “An organization has costs that change when its activity level rises or falls and costs that remain relatively stable in the short term. Materials consumed for each product tend to increase with the number of products, while rent may remain the same within a certain range. This classification does not say whether a cost is important or unimportant. Some costs have mixed components, such as a fixed fee plus a component proportional to usage. The classification helps estimate how expenses change when the activity level changes.”\n\nMaximum 650 words." "reference" $v8158 "conversion" $v8159 "profiles" $v8160 "tags" $v8161 "constraints" $v8162 "reviewNotes" "Source policy: development-visible"
@v8164 kernel.value.record "collection" "eslm-everyday-source.jsonl" "locator" "dataset.jsonl:908" "sourceId" "RO-091-08" "sourceDigest" "sha256:edd53ebd901f79c8fd487e501737edf2fa6d0537163a65c933b084dea9a4eacb" "sourceFile" "cases/benchmark_091.md"
