@v8230 kernel.value.record "answer" "The three views share one theme: there is tension between **uninterrupted work time** and **the need for rapid coordination**.\n\n**Agreements.** A and B accept the value of meeting-free focus time but want protection for emergencies. C adds that needs differ by team. All three give reasons to avoid an absolute rule.\n\n**The main disagreement** concerns how uniform the policy should be. C calls for adaptation by team type, while B warns that merely moving meetings to the next day would not be a real improvement.\n\n**Four-week pilot:** designate one day per week as “no scheduled meetings” by default, with exceptions for incidents, urgent client situations, and required short coordination in teams such as support. Organizers should review meetings and decide whether they can be canceled, shortened, handled asynchronously, or moved—not automatically shifted to the next day.\n\nMetrics: 1) total meeting hours per person per week; 2) number of meetings moved to the next day; 3) self-reported uninterrupted work time; 4) incidents or urgent problems reported as delayed.\n\nAt the end, discuss results separately by team type. The pilot produces data; it does not guarantee that the rule is useful.\n\nFor the pilot to be interpretable, communicate the rule before it starts and keep it relatively stable for all four weeks. If exceptions change every week, comparisons become difficult. The eventual decision may differ by team: the same policy could help analytics and have a neutral or negative effect on support. That difference would be a valid result, not a failed experiment. The comparison should use the same definitions throughout the four weeks." "requiredConcepts" $v8228 "forbiddenClaims" $v8229
@v8231 kernel.value.record "method" "manual-reviewed" "reviewed" true "note" "English wording was reviewed against the source response-length metadata."
@v8232 kernel.seq.make item "core-only"
