@v8397 kernel.value.record "format" "eslm-basic-everyday-eval-case" "id" "everyday-0933" "source" $v8389 "pool" "source-development" "category" "professional-communication" "difficulty" "easy" "scoring" "semantic" "prompt" "You want to ask for clarification of a deadline." "reference" $v8392 "conversion" $v8393 "profiles" $v8394 "tags" $v8395 "constraints" $v8396 "reviewNotes" "Source policy: development-visible"
@v8398 kernel.value.record "collection" "eslm-everyday-source.jsonl" "locator" "dataset.jsonl:934" "sourceId" "RO-094-04" "sourceDigest" "sha256:f675364639f3cdf36a340712e1bd78c2b87d35b28670a3fb7e2754278da00282" "sourceFile" "cases/benchmark_094.md"
@v8399 kernel.seq.make
@v8400 kernel.seq.make
@v8401 kernel.value.record "answer" "The feedback can be grouped into four themes: speed, clarity and specificity, continuity between agents, and consistency of information.\n\n**Strengths.** The most frequent positive feedback is speed, with 14 mentions. Clarity has 9 mentions, and friendly tone has 3.\n\n**Problems.** Repeating information after a transfer appears in 6 comments. Overly generic initial responses appear in 5, and long evening waits in 4. Contradictory information appears in 2 cases, but it may have greater impact than its frequency suggests.\n\n**Priority 1: continuity during transfers**, because it is the most frequently mentioned problem. Check whether the receiving agent can see the history and information already supplied.\n\n**Priority 2: specificity of the first response.** The 5 mentions suggest that speed is not sufficient if the response does not use the case context.\n\n**Priority 3: evening coverage and consistency.** Evening waits have 4 mentions; the 2 contradictory cases still deserve a separate investigation because they create a correctness risk.\n\nFollow-up questions: In which case types do problematic transfers occur? How much does response time differ in the evening? Which topics produced contradictory information?\n\nRanking by number of mentions is only a starting point. A rare issue such as contradictory information may be more serious than a frequent but minor one. The manager should therefore track frequency and severity separately. A simple cycle is to identify the theme, inspect concrete examples, make a small change, and measure whether complaints in that category decline." "requiredConcepts" $v8399 "forbiddenClaims" $v8400
@v8402 kernel.value.record "method" "manual-reviewed" "reviewed" true "note" ""
@v8403 kernel.seq.make item "core-only"
@v8404 kernel.seq.make item "assigned-train-visible" item "source-category:complex-everyday-task" item "domain:decision-and-synthesis" item "length:long"
@v8405 kernel.value.record "expectedMinWords" 230 "expectedMaxWords" 850 "acceptableVariations" "The answer may vary but must follow every constraint, use the supplied data, and not invent material information."
@v8406 kernel.value.record "format" "eslm-basic-everyday-eval-case" "id" "everyday-0934" "source" $v8398 "pool" "source-development" "category" "complex-everyday-task" "difficulty" "medium-hard" "scoring" "semantic" "prompt" "In one week, a customer-support team received the following feedback: 14 customers say responses are fast, 9 say explanations are clear, 6 complain that they must repeat the same information after being transferred between agents, 5 say the initial response was too generic, 4 complain about long evening wait times, 3 appreciate the friendly tone, and 2 say they received contradictory information from two agents.\n\nGroup the observations into themes, separate strengths from problems, prioritize no more than three improvements, and justify the order using only the supplied data. End with three follow-up questions for the manager. Maximum 750 words." "reference" $v8401 "conversion" $v8402 "profiles" $v8403 "tags" $v8404 "constraints" $v8405 "reviewNotes" "Source policy: development-visible"
