@v8479 kernel.value.record "collection" "eslm-everyday-source.jsonl" "locator" "dataset.jsonl:943" "sourceId" "RO-095-03" "sourceDigest" "sha256:e4e46cebebcc3c836f0e59789bb537f53e2967539f067fffe838f36168d73d2f" "sourceFile" "cases/benchmark_095.md"
@v8480 kernel.seq.make
@v8481 kernel.seq.make
@v8482 kernel.value.record "answer" "**Status.** Version 2.1 is nearly ready functionally: the main features are complete and the budget remains on track. A performance problem and two interface issues remain.\n\n**Risks.** The main risk is degraded performance under high load: an approximately 25% slowdown above 5,000 simultaneous users. Cache optimization is a proposed remedy, not a confirmed solution. There is also a commercial risk because the Beta client is requesting the original date.\n\n**Decisions already made.** The performance test will be repeated Thursday. The final launch decision will be made Friday at 13:00. If a delay is chosen, Beta will be informed the same day. The product team will accept no more than one week of delay.\n\n**Still undecided.** It has not been decided whether the September 18 launch remains in place, and it has not been shown that cache optimization solves the problem.\n\n**Next actions.** Complete the optimization, repeat testing, track the interface issues, and prepare Friday’s go/no-go decision. If the result leads to delay, communicate with Beta immediately.\n\nThe project is close to ready, but the launch date depends on performance validation.\n\nAn explicit performance acceptance criterion would help Friday’s decision because the notes do not say what level is sufficient. That is an information gap, not a reason to invent a threshold. Management should distinguish between launch-blocking problems, temporarily acceptable problems, and issues that can be fixed after launch, using criteria established by the team." "requiredConcepts" $v8480 "forbiddenClaims" $v8481
@v8483 kernel.value.record "method" "manual-reviewed" "reviewed" true "note" ""
@v8484 kernel.seq.make item "core-only"
