@v8587 kernel.value.record "collection" "eslm-everyday-source.jsonl" "locator" "dataset.jsonl:955" "sourceId" "RO-096-05" "sourceDigest" "sha256:0e0c661b7fa2730d9eed622ef1220e7d87ead54c7f01b30b0cbce67cd5a3a0b0" "sourceFile" "cases/benchmark_096.md"
@v8588 kernel.seq.make
@v8589 kernel.seq.make
@v8590 kernel.value.record "answer" "All three options fit the maximum budget, so the choice depends mainly on commute, space, and flexibility.\n\n**A** has the best office location: a 10-minute walk. Its disadvantages are the smallest area, no elevator, and a 12-month lease that fits poorly with uncertainty after 8 months.\n\n**B** is the least expensive and largest: EUR 460 and 52 m². It also has the most flexible lease, at 6 months. Its disadvantage is the 35-minute public-transport commute, repeated four days each week.\n\n**C** remains within budget and offers 45 m², an elevator, and a 20-minute bicycle commute. Parking has little value because you do not own a car. The 12-month lease remains a problem.\n\n**Recommendation: B.** Its combination of space, cost, and flexibility reduces contractual risk and fits the preference for a larger apartment. The main tradeoff is commute time.\n\nThe missing information that could change the decision is the commute’s real quality: service frequency, required transfers, and rush-hour duration. If A’s landlord accepted a lease of about 8 months, A would become much more attractive because it is so close to the office.\n\nA useful final check is to rank the criteria explicitly: flexibility, commute, space, then cost. This reduces the risk of choosing by price alone. The recommendation does not claim B is universally best; it is simply the closest fit to the stated preferences. If a preference changes—for example, if commute becomes dominant—the conclusion should be recalculated. Parking should not outweigh the stated preferences, and each twelve-month lease remains longer than the known eight-month horizon." "requiredConcepts" $v8588 "forbiddenClaims" $v8589
@v8591 kernel.value.record "method" "manual-reviewed" "reviewed" true "note" "English wording was reviewed against the source response-length metadata."
@v8592 kernel.seq.make item "core-only"
@v8593 kernel.seq.make item "assigned-train-visible" item "source-category:complex-everyday-task" item "domain:decision-and-synthesis" item "length:long"
@v8594 kernel.value.record "expectedMinWords" 256 "expectedMaxWords" 850 "acceptableVariations" "The answer may vary but must follow every constraint, use the supplied data, and not invent material information."
@v8595 kernel.value.record "format" "eslm-basic-everyday-eval-case" "id" "everyday-0955" "source" $v8587 "pool" "source-development" "category" "complex-everyday-task" "difficulty" "medium-hard" "scoring" "semantic" "prompt" "I need to choose among three apartments to rent. A: EUR 520/month, 38 m², a 10-minute walk from the office, fourth floor without an elevator, minimum 12-month lease. B: EUR 460/month, 52 m², 35 minutes by public transport, second floor with an elevator, minimum 6-month lease. C: EUR 590/month, 45 m², 20 minutes by bicycle, third floor with an elevator, minimum 12-month lease, and parking included.\n\nI work from the office four days a week, do not own a car, prefer more space, but care greatly about commute time. My maximum budget is EUR 600. I do not know whether I will remain in the city after the next 8 months.\n\nCompare the options by cost, space, commute, flexibility, and amenities. Recommend exactly one, state the main tradeoff, and identify missing information that could change the choice. Maximum 700 words." "reference" $v8590 "conversion" $v8591 "profiles" $v8592 "tags" $v8593 "constraints" $v8594 "reviewNotes" "Source policy: development-visible"
@v8596 kernel.value.record "collection" "eslm-everyday-source.jsonl" "locator" "dataset.jsonl:956" "sourceId" "RO-096-06" "sourceDigest" "sha256:f7be8aa12070f4ba39bf26f54c4748a661ec0cda6fea8f01bfb2f7b9bf7eb2a3" "sourceFile" "cases/benchmark_096.md"
