@v8713 kernel.value.record "collection" "eslm-everyday-source.jsonl" "locator" "dataset.jsonl:969" "sourceId" "RO-097-09" "sourceDigest" "sha256:67856a5e403f2c94bbe71c35c33e15129a8f461840f17a0431833392903cd2f6" "sourceFile" "cases/benchmark_097.md"
@v8714 kernel.seq.make
@v8715 kernel.seq.make
@v8716 kernel.value.record "answer" "The total workload is 6 + 4 + 8 + 3 + 5 + 2 = **28 hours**. The team’s theoretical capacity is 5 × 6 × 5 = **150 hours**, so total capacity is sufficient; the main concern is managing dependencies.\n\nA simple allocation:\n- Person 1: A on day one, then B, followed by F.\n- Person 2: C, started immediately and continued until complete.\n- Person 3: E on day one.\n- Person 4: D after C is complete.\n- Person 5: reserve for unexpected work or other team tasks.\n\nThe chains to track are A → B → F and C → D.\n\nIf C takes 4 extra hours, its duration becomes **12 hours**. The same person can finish it over two six-hour days, after which D can start the next day. Since D takes only 3 hours and there is substantial capacity buffer, the delay does not threaten completion within five days.\n\nThe plan does not artificially occupy every person; it uses excess capacity as a buffer for variation.\n\nA simple monitoring tool is a two-column list labeled “task” and “blocked by.” Check A and C first each morning because they open the dependency chains. If a delay occurs, update the affected path rather than needlessly reallocating independent tasks. The large capacity surplus is useful precisely as a reserve for variation. The reserve person does not need a task unless circumstances change." "requiredConcepts" $v8714 "forbiddenClaims" $v8715
@v8717 kernel.value.record "method" "manual-reviewed" "reviewed" true "note" "English wording was reviewed against the source response-length metadata."
